<PolicyLayout title="Privacy" description="How BlogStack handles account information, publishing content and optional visitor analytics.">
 <h2 id="website-cookies">This website and Google Analytics</h2><p>On the BlogStack marketing website, optional Google Analytics 4 helps us understand page visits and navigation after you accept analytics. Google may process browser/device information and set first-party analytics cookies. We disable advertising personalization and Google signals, send page URLs without query strings or fragments, and reduce referrers to their origin. We do not send setup form values, account information, private drafts or payment details to Google Analytics.</p><p>The tag is blocked until consent. Do Not Track and Global Privacy Control keep optional analytics off. Your choice is stored locally for 180 days. Use Cookie preferences in the footer to change it; see the <a href="/cookies">cookie policy</a> for details and links to Google's data practices. Choosing Essential only does not restrict access to the website.</p>
 <h2>Account and publishing data</h2><p>We process your account name, email, authentication records, workspace membership, content, media and subscription status to provide BlogStack. You retain ownership of your content. Private drafts are restricted to authorized project members.</p>
 <h2>Optional reader analytics on your blog</h2><p>When a website enables and installs tracking, BlogStack collects article views, article-link clicks, referrer domains and destination URLs without query strings. The analytics dataset does not store raw IP addresses or full user agents. Request IPs are used transiently for rate limiting, and user-agent text is used to filter common bots.</p><p>The tracker does not set analytics cookies. It respects Do Not Track, Global Privacy Control and the visitor opt-out setting. Project owners can disable tracking. Website owners remain responsible for their own notices and consent requirements.</p>
 <h2>AI processing</h2><p>When you request AI assistance, your instructions and relevant authorized article content are processed through Cloudflare Workers AI. Requests and proposals are stored in your workspace for review. An assistant cannot publish or apply changes independently.</p>
 <h2>Service providers</h2><p>Cloudflare provides application hosting, database, media storage, event analytics and AI processing. Resend handles transactional email. Polar handles checkout and subscription payments; BlogStack stores payment references and status rather than card numbers. Customer-supplied comments and newsletter providers have their own accounts and privacy policies.</p>
 <h2>Retention and export</h2><p>Analytics reports retain 30, 180 or 365 days according to the plan. At trial or subscription expiry, editing stops, public delivery continues for 30 days and content/media exports remain available for up to 90 days. Advance emails warn before delivery suspension and deletion. Billing records may be retained where required for accounting and payment reconciliation.</p><p>Customer websites may keep previously downloaded or independently built copies after BlogStack delivery stops. Deleted media stops being served immediately; storage cleanup can take 48 hours. Private database backups are retained for 30 days, or 90 days for Sunday snapshots. Backup media versions expire 90 days after their last snapshot reference.</p>
 <h2>Access, correction and deletion requests</h2><p>Use the dashboard export to retrieve workspace content and media. Contact support for account data access, correction or deletion requests. Include the account email; we verify authorization before disclosing or deleting private information.</p>
</PolicyLayout>
